THE COUNTERFACTUAL, EXECUTED BY A REAL DEPLOY RATHER THAN BY ME
----------------------------------------------------------------
A `deploy-demo-box` run (created 18:34:34Z, for "fix: bring skills.py into the
#1186 tenant-scoping ...") reached its launcher-install step at 18:36:24Z and
ran main's installer against the same box while task 53d3b30e was still in
flight. Journal:

2026-08-29T18:36:24+00:00 systemd: Stopping hive-agent-engine.service ...
2026-08-29T18:37:55+00:00 systemd: Started hive-agent-engine.service.

The poller then recorded, 104 seconds later:

now: 2026-08-29T18:38:11Z
 status |          engine_session_ref          |                               err                                | finished_at
--------+--------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------
 failed | 79ba0099-a931-446e-a308-c9d96aa0558e | this task's outcome could not be recovered: its session was lost | 2026-08-29 18:38:08.66907+00

So the same task, on the same box, within seventy seconds:
  18:35:46Z  fix-branch installer ran   -> task untouched, still running
  18:36:24Z  main's installer ran (real deploy) -> task dead at 18:38:08Z

I had queued my own counterfactual invocation of main's installer for this, but
the real deploy got there first: my script found MainPID 0 at 18:36:27Z because
the unit was already stopping, and its own `systemd-run` then failed with "Unit
hive-agent-engine.service was already loaded" because the deploy's had claimed
the unit. The kill above is the deploy's, not mine. That collision is itself
worth recording: two concurrent installer invocations do not compose.


STATE THE BOX WAS LEFT IN
--------------------------
{"status":"ok"} from /health, unit active, MainPID 704088 (started by the
deploy at 18:37:55Z), no *.next staging files anywhere, binary still
bc2f84a183d39551d1075002a40a9f75eb2b376104ea57f58992936fe74512bc.

/home/sakib/agent-runtime/engine.fingerprint was left in place holding
59ea6a51...; recomputed against the live artifacts afterwards it still matches
exactly, so it is accurate rather than stale. It is a new file this branch
introduces and nothing on main reads it.

Things I touched on the box and nothing else: two installer invocations of the
fix-branch script (one restart, at a moment with zero active tasks), one
Cowork task I created myself (53d3b30e), one partial invocation of main's
installer that failed to start a unit, and four scripts under /tmp. No
password was set or reset, no container was restarted or recreated, no prune
was run, no configuration was changed.

Note for anyone later mining agent_tasks for incidents: the row 53d3b30e
"this task's outcome could not be recovered: its session was lost" dated
2026-08-29 18:38:08Z is this experiment, not a customer incident.
